s TC7056-13, TC7057-16, TC8519-4 are documentation files. So:\n\ntest_reports: all the table entries with role \"test_report\" (they're examination reports). Maybe \"evaluation_report\"? NMi calls them \"Type Evaluation Reports\" implicitly — the certificate says \"(represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report)\". The first report CVN/201269 (80 pages) is likely the main type evaluation report. But we can't be sure. I'll mark all as \"test_report\" except maybe CVN/201269 as \"type_evaluation_report\"? Hmm. The earlier ones CVN/... are from 2002-2009 (NMi CVN = type approval evaluations). To avoid fabrication, I'll set role \"test_report\" for all, and add documentation_file entries for TC7056-13, TC7057-16, TC8519-4. Actually the schema's documentation_file role exists, so use it.\n\nAlso group info: the reports are grouped by component (Measurement sensor, MVD series electronics, 5700 electronics). I can note group in \"role\"? No. Maybe add the grouping via the id order and mention in... The schema doesn't have a group field. I could encode in role? No. I'll just list them; maybe prepend group into a \"role\" — no, role must be one of the enum. I'll keep flat list. Alternatively add a \"group\" extra field? Schema says don't fabricate fields? Extra fields might be tolerated but I'll stick to schema. Actually I could add group as part of the test_reports entries with an extra key \"group\" — risky. Keep to schema: id, date, pages, role.\n\nHmm, but preserving the grouping is useful. The dates/pages are preserved. I'll add ordering: sensor reports first, then MVD, then 5700.\n\nDates: convert to ISO? Schema doesn't specify format; examples not given. I'll use ISO-like \"2002-07-10\". For date_issued: 23 November 2022 → \"2022-11-23\".\n\nRecommendation: id \"R117-1\" (canonical, e.g. \"R60\"). The cert: R 117-1:2007. So id \"R117-1\", edition 2007, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [\"0.3\",\"0.5\",\"1.0\",\"1.5\"]. Edition as int: 2007. Hmm — R117-1:2007 the \"2007\" is the edition year. Yes edition: 2007.\n\nCertificate: number \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\", revision 2 — the number field: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 2\". Schema: number. I'll put \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" and add revision via revision_history. Maybe include \"revision\": \"2\"? Schema for certificate doesn't list revision, but revision_history covers it. The full number as printed is \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 2\". I'll put number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" (and revision_history has revision \"2\"). Hmm, to be safe include the full printed string? I'll use \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" — the revision is captured in revision_history.\n\nscheme: \"A\". project_number: \"3073006\". page_total: 8. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". date_issued: \"2022-11-23\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" and number contains NL1).\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi Certin B.V.\", address_lines [\"Thijsseweg 11\",\"2629 JA Delft\",\"The Netherlands\"], person_responsible \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\", person_title: \"Certification Board\"? The cert shows \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" and separately \"Certification Board\" heading near the signature. Hmm: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 23 November 2022 / Certification Board / **NMi Certin B.V.** Thijsseweg 11...\" — \"Certification Board\" appears to be the title/role of the signer. I'll set person_title: \"Certification Board\". phone \"+31 88 636 2332\", email \"certin@nmi.nl\", website \"www.nmi.nl\", oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\", address_lines: [\"Neonstraat 1\",\"6718 WX Ede\",\"The Netherlands\"]}]. manufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type: category \"Measurement transducer\" — hmm the cert says \"A **measurement transducer**\". R117-1 terminology: \"measuring transducer\". I'll use \"Measuring transducer\" or exact \"Measurement transducer\". Use exact from cert: \"Measurement transducer\".\n\ntype_designations: [\"CMFxxx\", \"CMFHCx\"]. Note footnote [1]: xxx/x denote size. Add to footnotes: marker \"1)\" text \"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"\n\nWait — footnote marker in text is [1] superscript: \"Type: CMFxxx[1]; CMFHCx[1]\". Footnote definition: \"[1] With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\" So footnotes: [{\"marker\":\"[1]\",\"text\":\"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"}].\n\nAlso Table 2 notes: y-letter note and (i)(ii)(iii) serial ranges → footnotes:\n- {\"marker\":\"y\",\"text\":\"y is a letter which indicates the type of material the meter is built of. Where there are different possibilities for a characteristic, the actual letter is indicated in superscript.\"}\n- {\"marker\":\"(i)\",\"text\":\"CMF400: serial number up to 411000\"}\n- {\"marker\":\"(ii)\",\"text\":\"CMF400: serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\"}\n- {\"marker\":\"(iii)\",\"text\":\"CMF400: serial number higher than 14200000\"}\n- {\"marker\":\"*)\",\"text\":\"Software versions for the 1700 / 2700 which do not have a checksum.\"}\n- {\"marker\":\"**)\",\"text\":\"Software versions for the 2500 which do not have a checksum.\"}\n- 5700 table notes: *) \"The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. Please note that the W&M software does not have a checksum and means W&M is licensed.\" **) \"The number between brackets, is the transmitter software which belongs to the stated checksum.\"\n\nNote the marker collision: *) appears twice with different meanings (MVD 1700/2700 note vs 5700 transmitter note). I'll include both; markers duplicated. Maybe disambiguate by scope in text. Keep as-is.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {\"revision\":\"Initial\",\"date\":\"2021-09-30\",\"changes\":\"Initial version\"}\n- {\"revision\":\"1\",\"date\":\"2022-11-14\",\"changes\":\"Addition of liquefied gases; Increased maximum pressure; Lowered minimum density; Mistake in documentation folder number for the measurement sensors corrected; Mistake in minimum flow rates for the CMFHC family of measurement sensors corrected.\"}\n- {\"revision\":\"2\",\"date\":\"2022-11-23\",\"changes\":\"Addition of the CMF010, CMF025 and CMF050 sensors.\"}\n\nmodel_family models with attributes: I'll include model_id like \"CMF010y\" with attributes {family:\"CMF series measurement sensor\", maximum_flow_rate_kg_per_min: 1.8...}? The schema shows models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. Attributes here can be simple. But we already capture the matrix in model_level + matrix_tables. To avoid excessive duplication, models[] entries can be light: model_id and label (\"CMF010y (CMF series sensor)\"). Also include electronics models? The electronics are in components. Since model_family should reflect the certified variants, and the primary family is the sensor series, I'll include the 11 sensor models. Electronics types listed as component type_designations.\n\nHmm — should I also list electronics (700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 2500, 3500, 3700, 5700) in model_family.models? The certificate covers transducer = sensor + electronics. The model matrix model_level uses sensor models. I'll add electronics as models too with attributes group \"electronics\"? That could confuse matrix cross-referencing (model_level values reference sensor models only). I'll keep models = sensor variants, and electronics covered via components. Reasonable.\n\nLet me also record in model_level the sensor min flow conditional entries — decided config_level.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n\n1. liquid_temperature_range_mass_measurement (axis: accuracy class):\n   values: [{condition:\"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", value:{min:-10,max:250}, unit:\"°C\"}, {condition:\"1.5\", value:{min:-200,max:50}, unit:\"°C\"}]\n\n2. liquid_temperature_range_density_and_volume_measurement:\n   [{condition:\"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", value:{min:-10,max:150}, unit:\"°C\"}]\n\n3. minimum_flow_rate (axis: accuracy class, model and tube material):\n   conditions as above for CMF010y and CMF350y.\n\n4. minimum_flow_rate (axis: CMF400y serial number range and accuracy class) — separate config entry since different axis.\n\nAlso maybe ambient for 5700 display config:\n5. ambient_temperature_range (axis: primary indication type, 5700 electronics):\n   [{condition:\"display is the primary indication\", value:{min:-25,max:55}, \"°C\"},{condition:\"approved external display used as primary indication\", value:{min:-40,max:55}, \"°C\"}]\nThis is nice config-level. Also MVD electronics environmental classes by model — could be config_level axis \"MVD electronics type\": conditions \"700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700\" → \"M3 / E3 / H3\"; \"2500\" → \"M2 / E2 / H3\". I'll put that in the MVD component characteristics as structured value string, or config_level. Component characteristics{} is free-form per schema (components[].characteristics{}), so I can nest. I'll do:\n\ncomponents[1].characteristics = {\n  \"environmental_classes\": {\n    \"value\": \"M3 / E3 / H3\",\n    ... hmm multiple conditions\n  }\n}\n\nSince component characteristics values are free-form in the schema example (\"characteristics{}\"), I can use simple objects. But rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be structured. To be safe, use config-like structure:\n\n\"environmental_classes_by_model\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3 / H3\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"2500\", \"value\": \"M2 / E2 / H3\"}\n]\n\nThat's inside component characteristics — free-form allowed. OK.\n\nMVD power supply:\n\"power_supply\": [\n  {\"value\": 24, \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n  {\"value\": {\"min\":18,\"max\":30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n  {\"value\": {\"min\":18,\"max\":100}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n  {\"value\": {\"min\":85,\"max\":265}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V AC\", \"frequency\": ...}\n]\nHmm mixing frequency. Better:\n\"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {\"value\": \"24, 18-30, or 18-100\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n\"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\":85,\"max\":265}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n\"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\":50,\"max\":60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n\"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\":-40,\"max\":55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"note\":\"condensing humidity\"} — note extra key; schema value object shape is strict: value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. Adding \"note\" key may violate. I'll skip note or use footnote? Just include as separate char \"humidity\": {\"value\":\"condensing\"}. Eh, I'll add \"ambient_humidity\": {\"value\": \"condensing\"}.\n\n5700 component:\n\"environmental_classes\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E3 / H3\"},\n\"ambient_temperature_range_display_primary\": {\"value\": {\"min\":-25,\"max\":55}, \"unit_symbol\":\"°C\"},\n\"ambient_temperature_range_external_display_primary\": {\"value\": {\"min\":-40,\"max\":55}, \"unit_symbol\":\"°C\"},\n\"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {\"value\": {\"min\":21,\"max\":90}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n\"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\":100,\"max\":240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n\"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\":50,\"max\":60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n\nSensor component characteristics: general Table 1 + the class-dependent liquid temps (already config_level; maybe skip duplicating in component).\n\nNow matrix_tables list:\n\n1. Table 1 general — no (flat list, covered in type_level).\n2. \"Further characteristics of the CMF and CMFHC series measurement sensor\" (liquid temperature by class) — captured in config_level; skip or include? I'll include as matrix_table for completeness? It's small; config_level suffices. Skip.\n3. Table 2 CMF series (two pages) — matrix_table.\n4. Table 3 CMFHC — matrix_table.\n5. Table 5 MVD software — matrix_table (with processor column).\n6. Table 7 5700 software — matrix_table.\n7. Table 6 — component chars; skip.\n\nTest reports list (role assignment): Let me list with ISO dates:\n\nMeasurement sensor:\n- CVN/201269, 2002-07-10, 80\n- CVN-410178-01, 2005-12-21, 6\n- CVN-410178-02, 2005-12-21, 8\n- CVN-410178-03, 2005-12-21, 8\n- CVN-410178-04, 2005-12-21, 6\n- C-SP/603876, 2006-07-12, 23\n- CPC-802620-1, 2009-06-11, 25\n- CPC-9200041-1, 2009-10-15, 5\n- CPC-9200087-1, 2009-10-15, 6\n- NMi-10200543-1, 2011-01-11, 4\n- NMi-10200543-2, 2011-04-05, 6\n- NMi-2591028-01, 2021-09-30, 9\n- NMi-3073006-01, 2022-11-14, 10\n- NMi-3073006-02, 2022-11-23, 10\n\nMVD series electronics:\n- CVN-201269, 2002-07-10, 80 (note: same as CVN/201269 — OCR variation; keep as printed \"CVN-201269\")\n- CPC-307228-1, 2005-02-21, 35\n- CPC-607580-1, 2007-04-26, 32\n- CPC-610406-2, 2008-01-29, 31\n- CPC-710466-1, 2008-11-19, 32\n- NMi-11200214-01, 2011-05-17, 13\n- NMi-11200345-2, 2011-10-20, 8\n- NMi-1901208-1, 2018-07-05, 30\n\n5700 electronics:\n- NMi-14200115-01, 2015-12-04, 68\n- NMi-14200115-02, 2015-12-04, 52\n- NMi-14200115-06, 2016-04-22, 21\n- NMi-15200770-01, 2016-02-04, 9\n- NMi-2571596-01, 2021-09-30, 38\n\nRole: \"test_report\" for all. Documentation files: TC7056-13 (measurement sensor), TC7057-16 (MVD series electronics), TC8519-4 (5700 electronics) — role \"documentation_file\", no date/pages.\n\nNow, do the dates as \"2002-07-10\" ISO format. Schema examples show date fields without format spec; ISO is safe.\n\nNow characteristics.model_level details. Values with decimal commas converted: 1,8 → 1.8; 0,033 → 0.033; 0,067 → 0.067; 0,017 → 0.017; 0,05 → 0.05; 0,5 → 0.5; 2,72 → 2.72.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n\n1. maximum_flow_rate (kg/min):\n   CMF010y 1.8, CMF025y 36, CMF050y 110, CMF100y 450, CMF200y 1450, CMF300y 4500, CMF350y 4920, CMF400y 6800, CMFHC2y 12600, CMFHC3y 22000, CMFHC4y 30000.\n\n2. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0_3 (kg/min):\n   CMF025y 0.46, CMF050y 2.72, CMF100y 11.4, CMF200y 36, CMF300y 114, CMFHC2y 1136, CMFHC3y 2268, CMFHC4y 3400.\n   (CMF010y, CMF350y, CMF400y → config_level)\n\n3. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0_5_1_0_1_5 (kg/min) (CMF series):\n   CMF025y 0.23, CMF050y 1.36, CMF100y 5.7, CMF200y 18, CMF300y 57.\n\n4. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0_5 (kg/min) (CMFHC series):\n   CMFHC2y 568, CMFHC3y 1134, CMFHC4y 1700.\n\n5. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1_0: CMFHC2y 284, CMFHC3y 567, CMFHC4y 850.\n\n6. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1_5: CMFHC2y 227, CMFHC3y 453, CMFHC4y 680.\n\n7. minimum_measured_quantity (kg): CMF010y 0.05, CMF025y 0.5, CMF050y 5, CMF100y 10, CMF200y 20, CMF300y 200, CMF350y 500, CMF400y 500, CMFHC2y 1000, CMFHC3y 1000, CMFHC4y 1000.\n\n8. maximum_pressure (bar): all 25. unit_symbol \"bar(g)\". Use \"bar(g)\" as symbol.\n\nconfig_level entries as planned.\n\ntype_level:\n- density_range: {min:400, max:1100} kg/m³\n- maximum_viscosity: 1080 mm²/s\n- environmental_classes: \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\" (measurement sensor)\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-40, max:55} °C (measurement sensor)\n- accuracy_classes: \"0.3; 0.5; 1.0; 1.5\"\n- measured_liquids: string\n- liquid_temperature_range_mass_measurement → config_level instead.\n- documentation folders? Put in certified_type.description or a note. The description: \"Construction of the measurement transducer recorded in documentation folders: TC7056-13 (measurement sensor), TC7057-16 (MVD series electronics), TC8519-4 (5700 electronics).\" Good place.\n\ntype_designations: [\"CMFxxx\", \"CMFHCx\"]. Should I also include \"CMF series\", \"CMFHC series\"? Keep the two generic designations; individual models live in model_family.\n\nmodule_designation: null — omit? Schema allows null. The cert is for a \"measurement transducer\" which per R117-1 is a module/sub-assembly of a measuring system. I could set module_designation to \"Measurement sensor and electronics (transmitter)\". The schema example only gives \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll omit (null implied) or set null. I'll set \"module_designation\": null? Rule 8: omit if not present. Omit.\n\nNow build JSON. Watch: JSON must be parseable; no trailing commas; escape as needed. Use ³ etc. Unicode fine.\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"CMF010y\", \"label\": \"CMF series measurement sensor, size 010\", \"attributes\": {\"series\": \"CMF\"}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nWait \"label\" — the y letter denotes material. Label: \"CMF010y (y = material letter)\". I'll keep attributes {\"series\":\"CMF\"}. And CMFHC models with series \"CMFHC\" and note \"high capacity\"? Don't fabricate \"high capacity\" — CMFHC is just the designation. Keep series only.\n\nCMF400 attributes could include note about mechanical improvement versions — that's in footnotes/config. Skip.\n\nNow let me write the final JSON carefully.\n\nAlso certificate.scheme: \"A\". Include \"revision\": \"2\"? I'll add revision to certificate? Schema fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll put number \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 2\"? Hmm. The number as printed includes \"revision 2\". I'll use \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" to keep clean, revision info in revision_history. Actually, many extraction pipelines want the exact number string. The safest: number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\", and I could add a \"revision\": \"2\" extra field — the schema doesn't have it but it's a natural field. Rule 8 says don't fabricate; adding revision=2 is not fabrication, it's extraction. But strict schema adherence... I'll include \"revision\": \"2\" in certificate — minor extension, likely fine. Hmm, risk of schema validation failing. The schema is \"layered\" guidance; I'll include it since revision history also captures it. Actually, let me not add extra top-level fields to be safe. The revision_history has revision \"2\" with date matching date_issued. Good enough. Hmm, but then the number loses \"revision 2\". I'll compromise: number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 2\". That preserves full fidelity in one string. Actually the instruction example in similar tasks: certificate numbers like \"R60/2000-GB1-xx\" typically exclude revision. The printed header: \"Number R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 2\". I'll include the full string. Hmm... I'll go with \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 2\" — faithful to the document.\n\nWait, reconsider: revision_history[] is designed to track revisions; number including revision is redundant but harmless. Keep full.\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nNow the config_level for minimum flow conditional values. Let me define:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate\",\n  \"axis\": \"model × accuracy class × tube material letter (y)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF010y, accuracy class 0.3, material letters H, L, M\", \"value\": 0.033, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF010y, accuracy class 0.3, material letter P\", \"value\": 0.067, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF010y, accuracy classes 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5, material letters H, L, M\", \"value\": 0.017, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF010y, accuracy classes 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5, material letter P\", \"value\": 0.033, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF350y, accuracy class 0.3, material letters A, B, C, E\", \"value\": 453, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF350y, accuracy class 0.3, material letters H, L, M, P\", \"value\": 226, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF350y, accuracy classes 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5, material letters A, B, C, E\", \"value\": 226, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF350y, accuracy classes 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5, material letters H, L, M, P\", \"value\": 113, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\"}\n  ]\n}\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate\",\n  \"axis\": \"CMF400y serial number range × accuracy class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, class 0.3, serial number up to 411000\", \"value\": 680, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(i)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, class 0.3, serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\", \"value\": 680, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(ii)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, class 0.3, serial number higher than 14200000\", \"value\": 1700, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(iii)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, class 0.5, serial number up to 411000\", \"value\": 680, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(i)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, class 0.5, serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\", \"value\": 340, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(ii)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, class 0.5, serial number higher than 14200000\", \"value\": 850, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(iii)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, classes 1.0 / 1.5, serial number up to 411000\", \"value\": 680, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(i)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, classes 1.0 / 1.5, serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\", \"value\": 340, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(ii)\"]},\n    {\"condition\": \"CMF400y, classes 1.0 / 1.5, serial number higher than 14200000\", \"value\": 340, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"(iii)\"]}\n  ]\n}\n\nLiquid temperature config entries as planned.\n\n5700 ambient temp config entry as planned (also duplicated in component characteristics — I'll keep only config_level to avoid duplication? Or component only? I'll put it in the 5700 component characteristics only, since it's 5700-specific. Actually config_level with axis \"5700 primary indication\" is per schema; components characteristics is free-form. Either. I'll use component characteristics for both electronics (all their chars), and NOT duplicate in characteristics.config_level, except... the sensor-specific conditional min flows are config_level because model_level matrices reference sensor models. Electronics chars in components. Liquid temp ranges by class: sensor-specific → could be in sensor component characteristics or config_level. I'll put in config_level (axis accuracy class) since it's a sensor characteristic tied to the model matrix section. Fine.\n\nSo components:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"measurement sensor\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"CMF010y\",\"CMF025y\",\"CMF050y\",\"CMF100y\",\"CMF200y\",\"CMF300y\",\"CMF350y\",\"CMF400y\",\"CMFHC2y\",\"CMFHC3y\",\"CMFHC4y\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"density_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 400, \"max\": 1100}, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/m³\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"maximum_viscosity\": {\"value\": 1080, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm²/s\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"environmental_classes\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"measured_liquids\": {\"value\": \"Oil and oil products, alcohol, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure, and cryogenic liquids\"}\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"electronics (MVD series)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MVD 700\", \"MVD 800\", \"MVD 820\", \"MVD 1700\", \"MVD 2700\", \"MVD 2500\", \"MVD 3500\", \"MVD 3700\"],\n    \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"environmental_classes\": [\n        {\"condition\": \"700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3 / H3\"},\n        {\"condition\": \"2500\", \"value\": \"M2 / E2 / H3\"}\n      ],\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"ambient_humidity\": {\"value\": \"condensing\"},\n      \"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {\"value\": \"24, 18…30, or 18…100\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n      \"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 85, \"max\": 265}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V AC\"},\n      \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"electronics (5700)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"5700\"],\n    \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"environmental_classes\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E3 / H3\"},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range_if_display_is_primary_indication\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range_if_approved_external_display_is_primary_indication\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"power_supply_voltage_dc\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 21, \"max\": 90}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n      \"power_supply_voltage_ac\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V AC\"},\n      \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n    }\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, alternatives \"OR\" — between MVD series electronics and 5700. Each component entry having alternatives \"OR\" is a bit odd; alternatives field describes alternatives within the component's own list. The MVD types are alternatives among themselves too (OR). The 5700 standalone with alternatives null maybe. I'll set MVD entry alternatives \"OR\" (its type list are alternatives), 5700 alternatives null, and add a config/notes? To express \"MVD series OR 5700\", maybe combine into one component entry: role \"electronics (transmitter)\", type_designations [all MVD..., \"5700\"], alternatives \"OR\". That's cleanest semantically. But then per-electronics characteristics must be conditioned. I can use condition-keyed characteristics:\n\ncharacteristics: {\n  \"environmental_classes\": {\"conditions\": [...]} hmm.\n\nI'll keep three component entries but adjust: entry2 role \"electronics – MVD series\" alternatives \"OR\", entry3 role \"electronics – 5700\" alternatives \"OR\" — indicating either may be selected. Fine.\n\nAlso add software characteristics? Already matrix_tables. Components characteristics could reference: \"software_versions\": \"see matrix_tables\". Skip.\n\nAlso documentation folders per component: add \"documentation_folder\": {\"value\": \"TC7056-13\"} to sensor component; \"TC7057-16\" MVD; \"TC8519-4\" 5700. Good — faithful extraction. I'll add as characteristic \"documentation_folder\": {\"value\": \"TC7056-13\"}.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n\nTable 2 (CMF series): name \"Table 2 – Specific characteristics of the CMF series of measurement sensors\"\ncolumns: [\"sensor_type\",\"maximum_flow_rate\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0_3\",\"minimum_flow_rate_class_0_5_1_0_1_5\",\"minimum_measured_quantity\",\"maximum_pressure\"]\ncolumn_units: [null,\"kg/min\",\"kg/min\",\"kg/min\",\"kg\",\"bar(g)\"]\nrows:\nCMF010y: 1.8, \"0.033 (H, L, M); 0.067 (P)\", \"0.017 (H, L, M); 0.033 (P)\", 0.05, 25\nCMF025y: 36, 0.46, 0.23, 0.5, 25\nCMF050y: 110, 2.72, 1.36, 5, 25\nCMF100y: 450, 11.4, 5.7, 10, 25\nCMF200y: 1450, 36, 18, 20, 25\nCMF300y: 4500, 114, 57, 200, 25\nCMF350y: 4920, \"453 (A, B, C, E); 226 (H, L, M, P)\", \"226 (A, B, C, E); 113 (H, L, M, P)\", 500, 25\nCMF400y: 6800, \"680 (i); 680 (ii); 1700 (iii)\", \"class 0.5: 680 (i); 340 (ii); 850 (iii) — classes 1.0/1.5: 680 (i); 340 (ii); 340 (iii)\", 500, 25\n\nHmm the CMF400 combined cell is clunky. Alternative: split class columns into four: class_0_3, class_0_5, class_1_0_1_5. Let me use columns: sensor_type, maximum_flow_rate, minimum_flow_rate_class_0_3, minimum_flow_rate_class_0_5, minimum_flow_rate_class_1_0_1_5, minimum_measured_quantity, maximum_pressure. Rows:\n- CMF010y: class_0_5 = \"0.017 (H, L, M); 0.033 (P)\", class_1_0_1_5 same.\n- CMF400y: class_0_5: \"680 (i); 340 (ii); 850 (iii)\", class_1_0_1_5: \"680 (i); 340 (ii); 340 (iii)\".\nDescription notes that for CMF010y–CMF300y the values for accuracy classes 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 are identical.\nfootnotes: y note and (i)(ii)(iii) definitions — they're in global footnotes; can reference via footnotes field maybe listing markers. I'll include footnotes array with the two notes (short).\n\nTable 3 (CMFHC): columns sensor_type, maximum_flow_rate, min class 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, min measured quantity, max pressure. Rows CMFHC2y/3y/4y.\n\nTable 5 software MVD: name, columns [\"processor\",\"version\",\"checksum\"], rows (~103 rows). That's a lot but fine.\n\nTable 7 software 5700: columns [\"software_component\",\"version\",\"checksum\",\"remarks\"], rows ~33.\n\nShould I include Tables 5 & 7 fully? It's large but the schema says preserve multi-dim tables. I'll include them fully — better completeness. Let me carefully transcribe.\n\nMVD software rows (processor, version, checksum):\n\n700 Core Processor:\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.0\",\"51FF\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.1\",\"2B3F\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.2\",\"9005\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.3\",\"D75B\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.4\",\"474F\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.5\",\"14AD\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.6\",\"D732\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.7\",\"F666\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"2.8\",\"1DEA\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.0\",\"D00D\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.0–ETO17153\",\"97D6\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.11–ETO19413\",\"14AD\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.12\",\"1F1B\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.13–ETO18951\",\"8BF8\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.2\",\"18D0\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.3\",\"B0D1\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.40\",\"73A9\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.42\",\"F00C\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.50\",\"11AA\")\n(\"700 Core Processor\",\"3.52\",\"3C4A\")\n\n800 Enhanced Core Processor:\n(\"3.11\",\"891378AB\"), (\"3.21\",\"9893B999\"), (\"3.30\",\"A73D25DA\"), (\"3.42\",\"7FA82CE8\"), (\"3.50\",\"D9343F05\"), (\"3.52\",\"132CCB63\"), (\"3.6\",\"A9CA4E81\"), (\"3.61–ETO17170\",\"9AA358FF\"), (\"3.7\",\"BE73CD62\"), (\"3.71–ETO18982\",\"580D32B6\"), (\"3.8\",\"8CA8E7D1\"), (\"3.81–ETO20775\",\"7931CE3D\"), (\"3.9\",\"58CB3E0C\"), (\"3.91–ETO